        "excerpt": "Department of Petroleum Engineering,  Heriot-Watt University\n43\n33\nGas Lift\n                        \nSandface flowingpressure\nWell production rate\n2.875\" Tubing OD\nWell inflow at\n40% Water cut\n3.5\"\n4.5\"\n5.5\"\n                        \nWell production rate\nTubing internal diameter (in)\n2             2.5             3             3.5              4             4.5              5\n3.5\"OD\n4.5\"OD\n5.5\"OD\n2.875\"OD\n2             2.5             3             3.5              4             4.5              5\n2             2.5             3             3.5              4             4.5              5\nWe showed in Chapter 1 (Well Performance) that separator pressure affected the\noutflow performance of naturally flowing wells.  The same is true for gas lifted wells\n- Figure 32 show how the well outflow performance improves as the separator\npressure is decreased.  This is shown explicitly in Figure 33, where the production rate\nis plotted as a function of separator pressure.  In general, the lower the separator\npressure, the higher the production rate.\n                       \nSandface flowing pressure\nWell production rate\nSeparator pressure (psi)\nWell inflow at\n40% Water cut\n250\n200\n150\n100\n50\n25"
